<<~ ahu #/head>>

! The swarm harvest

Four research waves went out and ran three levels deep. ''55 spirits wrote something worth keeping''; each carrier here holds ''that spirit's three largest messages, verbatim and unedited''.

''Names come from the spirits themselves.'' Each carrier takes its title from the heading its own spirit wrote, never from a summary somebody made afterward — so the roll below reads as the swarm described its own work.

⚠ ''WHY A LANE WITH NO NAME STILL EARNS A CARRIER.'' Most of these spirits were sub-lanes nobody addressed directly; their work reached the operator only through a parent's summary, and a summary is where a fabricated relay hid earlier in this same swarm. ''Holding the raw messages means a later reader can check a parent against its children'' rather than trusting the fold.

⚠ ''AND THE FENCE IS LOAD-BEARING.'' Raw transcript carries `<<~`, `>>` and code fences of its own. Each message rides a fence longer than any run of backticks inside it, so ''the grammar reads content where a naive write would read sigils'' — the same law the corpus already keeps: //an edge inside a fence is a lesson, never a link//.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-roll>>

